Karst is open source throughout, but it is not licensed uniformly. Which
license applies depends on which part of the tree a file is in.

    crates/**                        MIT OR Apache-2.0, at your option
    bins/**                          MIT OR Apache-2.0, at your option
    server/**                        AGPL-3.0-or-later
    web/**                           AGPL-3.0-or-later
    spec/**, docs/**                 CC-BY-4.0

Every source file carries an SPDX-License-Identifier in its first three lines.
Where a file header and the table above disagree, THE FILE HEADER WINS.

Full texts are in LICENSES/, named by SPDX identifier:

    LICENSES/MIT.txt
    LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt
    LICENSES/AGPL-3.0.txt
    LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt

LICENSING.md explains the reasoning, and ADR-0007
(docs/adr/0007-licensing.md) records what the arrangement costs.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

THE AGPL ON THE SERVER DOES NOT AFFECT YOUR USE OF THE CLIENT

This is the most common misreading of a split like this one, so to be explicit:

  - karstd, karst and karst-relay are MIT/Apache-2.0. Running, embedding,
    packaging, modifying or shipping them inside a proprietary product imposes
    no copyleft obligation of any kind.

  - The node agent and the coordination server are separate programs that
    communicate over a network protocol. Running an AGPL server does not make
    your client, your infrastructure, or anything else you run a derivative
    work of it.

  - The AGPL obligation is narrow: if you modify the coordination server or
    the console and let others use it over a network, you must offer those
    users the modified source. Running it unmodified obliges you to nothing
    beyond preserving notices.

The reason for that obligation is that the coordination server holds every
per-pair PSK and computes every node's packet filter. If your operator runs a
modified server you cannot inspect, you cannot verify the most
security-critical component in the system. The AGPL turns "trust your
operator" into "verify your operator."

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

THE PROTOCOL IS FREE TO IMPLEMENT

The PHREATIC and KARST-CONTROL specifications in spec/ are published under
CC-BY-4.0, with an irrevocable, royalty-free right to implement them in
software under any license, open or proprietary. Independent implementations
are welcome and actively wanted: a protocol with one implementation has not
been meaningfully reviewed.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

VENDORED CODE

server/ is a pruned fork of NetBird (netbirdio/netbird, v0.76.3, f65f7b34)
and carries its original licensing, which is itself split: management/,
signal/, relay/ and combined/ are AGPL-3.0-or-later, and the remainder is
BSD-3-Clause. See server/LICENSE-NETBIRD-BSD-3 and server/LICENSES/.

Copyright is held by the Karst contributors and, for the vendored tree, by the
NetBird authors. There is no CLA and no copyright assignment, so the project
cannot unilaterally relicense. That constraint is intentional.

